export const body = "<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>When you wander around a theme park, you will often see some big buildings or structures. These attractions are called \"landmarks\" or \"weenies.\" Their purpose is to provide you with a point of reference so that you can tell where you currently are in the environment. If you unfortunately get lost, you at least have a place to come back to.</p>\n<p>The purpose of landmarks remains largely identical in video games, but with a few extra benefits inherent to this interactive medium. In open-world games, landmarks are designed to encourage exploration whereas linear games usually use them to establish an objective for the player.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/4cc5b3d1201393d58b86.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/4cc5b3d1201393d58b86.webp\" width=\"514\" height=\"253\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/ee449aa088500d5e0a6c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/ee449aa088500d5e0a6c.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/ed745ea5a8134ac8adb3.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/ed745ea5a8134ac8adb3.webp\" width=\"792\" height=\"493\" style=\"--source-width:283px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/c067a1fd476bf27ba2f9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/c067a1fd476bf27ba2f9.webp\" width=\"834\" height=\"467\" style=\"--source-width:283px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>In her 2019 GDC talk, Raylene Deck exemplified the necessity of landmarks in open-world games through her experience with <em>Dragon Age: Inquisition</em>. When working on one area called \"Crestwood,\" she and the team received some feedback from internal playtesters saying, \"We were bored, and we couldn't find much to do,\" although there were actually plenty of things to do. The issue lied in the visibility of the content, and in Raylene's own words: \"Looking at that image, it's hard to know where to find the fun.\"</p>\n<p>So, the designers populated the level with various points of interest and reworked the composition. The iterated layout feels richer and presents to the player two goals: the short-term goal of exploring the small town, and the long-term goal of reaching the tower far away. This change resolved the visibility issue, and by the next playtest session, the complaint disappeared.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>The use of landmarks in linear games is less pronounced, but not at all absent. Level designers mainly use them to establish an objective, and over the course of the level, communicate progress to the player.</p>\n<p>For example, \"Chapter 13: Marooned\" in <em>Uncharted 4</em> begins with a cutscene that establishes a distant, jagged mountain as the destination. As you traverse through the level, this mountain will occasionally enters the frame, subtly informing you of your proximity to the objective.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/afffdc3f6782d6f94c70.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/afffdc3f6782d6f94c70.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"623\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/03fa3b508001bd825a5b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/03fa3b508001bd825a5b.webp\" width=\"661\" height=\"391\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/e7e0be57feee2bb769d2.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/e7e0be57feee2bb769d2.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"617\" style=\"--source-width:283px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/96bba9b04cc535449253.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/96bba9b04cc535449253.webp\" width=\"784\" height=\"439\" style=\"--source-width:283px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Landmarks are often big, but not always. Relying on the size as the sole parameter of your landmarks can diminish their visual uniqueness. The Great Plateau in <em>Breath of the Wild</em> is brimming with myriad attractions, each with a distinct silhouette and color. As seen in the bottom picture, even a lonely tree can stir our curiosity, so long as it stands out from the surroundings.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Similar to affordances and signifiers, you can add a landmark to your blockmesh to show your direction for the level. And make sure it's eye-catching not only because of the size.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/d87a071aa1182df8d937.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/d87a071aa1182df8d937.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"645\" style=\"--source-width:283px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/28bf9c42819599106014.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/28bf9c42819599106014.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"692\" style=\"--source-width:283px\" alt=\"Illustration 10\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>";
